      "name": "How are spiral conveyors supported?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Most spiral conveyors are self-supporting: a central column or drum and a ring of outer posts carry the helical track, and the base anchors to the floor. Tall units, units that pass through a mezzanine or floor opening, and units in seismic zones may need lateral bracing to building structure. Confirm floor slab capacity at the base, anchor requirements, and how the infeed and discharge ends are supported." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the difference between a spiral conveyor and a vertical reciprocating conveyor?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "A spiral conveyor provides continuous flow: products enter and exit simultaneously without stopping. A vertical reciprocating conveyor (VRC) lifts a batch of products on a platform, pauses to load and unload, and returns for the next batch. Spirals handle higher sustained throughput of cases and totes. VRCs handle heavier individual loads such as pallets and fit a smaller footprint. Spirals cost more but eliminate batch cycling delays." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How much does a spiral conveyor cost?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Spiral conveyors represent a significant capital investment compared to incline belt conveyors or VRCs. Cost depends on elevation change, belt width, tier count, drive type, environment (washdown, freezer), and construction material. Spiral conveyors justify their cost through continuous throughput and a small floor footprint." }
    }
