<BaseLayout title="Spiral Conveyors: Modular Systems, Supports &#x26; Selection" description="Vertical spiral conveyor systems explained: modular vs custom spirals, drive types, supports and structure, spiral vs VRC vs incline, and how to specify one." canonical="https://packagingconveyor.com/conveyor-types/spiral-conveyors/" schema={[breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema, articleSchema]}>

  <!-- Breadcrumb -->
  <nav class="bg-surface border-b border-border" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-3">
      <ol class="flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-ink-muted">
        <li><a href="/" class="hover:text-accent transition-colors">Home</a></li>
        <li class="text-ink-muted/50">/</li>
        <li><a href="/conveyor-types/" class="hover:text-accent transition-colors">Conveyor Types</a></li>
        <li class="text-ink-muted/50">/</li>
        <li class="text-ink font-semibold">Spiral Conveyors</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </nav>

  <!-- AIO -->
  <section class="py-16 sm:py-20 bg-surface-dim bg-dots">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="section-label text-accent mb-4">Vertical Spiral Conveyors</div>
      <h1 class="font-display text-5xl sm:text-6xl font-extrabold text-ink tracking-wide uppercase leading-tight mb-8">
        Spiral Conveyor Systems
      </h1>
      <div class="text-xl text-ink-muted leading-relaxed max-w-3xl space-y-4">
        <p>A spiral conveyor is a vertical conveyor that moves products between elevation levels along a helical path wrapped around a central column. Products enter and exit at the same time, so flow stays continuous with no batch cycling. Spirals serve <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-hover font-semibold">packaging lines</a>, distribution centers, and food plants where floor space is limited and throughput rules out batch lifts like <a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-reciprocating/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-hover font-semibold">vertical reciprocating conveyors (VRCs)</a>.</p>
        <p>The main buying decisions are the vertical conveyor type (spiral, VRC, or incline), modular or custom construction, drive type, and how the unit is supported. The comparison below covers the first.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Spiral vs VRC Comparison -->
  <section class="py-20 bg-surface" aria-labelledby="comparison-heading">
    <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="section-label text-accent mb-4">Comparison</div>
      <h2 id="comparison-heading" class="font-display text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold text-ink tracking-wide mb-10">
        Spiral vs. VRC vs. <span class="text-accent">Incline</span>
      </h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto -mx-4 px-4">
        <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse min-w-[700px]">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b-2 border-ink">
              <th class="font-display text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink py-4 pr-4">Feature</th>
              <th class="font-display text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink py-4 pr-4">Spiral Conveyor</th>
              <th class="font-display text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink py-4 pr-4">VRC (Reciprocating)</th>
              <th class="font-display text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink py-4">Incline Belt Conveyor</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-base text-ink-muted">
            <tr class="border-b border-border">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-ink">Flow Type</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Continuous</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Batch (stop-start)</td>
              <td class="py-3">Continuous</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-border">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-ink">Throughput</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">High, set by belt speed and spacing</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Limited by cycle time</td>
              <td class="py-3">High, set by belt speed and spacing</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-border">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-ink">Floor Space</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Compact round footprint</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Smallest (platform footprint)</td>
              <td class="py-3">Largest (long incline run)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-border">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-ink">Elevation Change</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Tall lifts in one footprint</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Tall lifts, multiple levels</td>
              <td class="py-3">Limited by incline angle and run length</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-border">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-ink">Typical Loads</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Cases, cartons, totes, packaged goods</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Pallets, carts, heavy loads</td>
              <td class="py-3">Cases, bags, loose product</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-border">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-ink">Best For</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">High-throughput cases and cartons</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Heavy loads, low throughput</td>
              <td class="py-3">Low lifts where floor length is available</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-ink">Relative Cost</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-sm">$$$$</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-sm">$$$</td>
              <td class="py-3 font-mono text-sm">$$</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted mt-4">See the <a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-reciprocating/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-hover font-semibold">VRC guide</a>, <a href="/conveyor-types/incline-conveyors/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-hover font-semibold">incline conveyor guide</a>, and <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-hover font-semibold">cost guide</a> for detail on each option.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- How Spirals Work -->
  <article class="py-20 bg-surface-dim bg-dots" aria-labelledby="how-heading">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="section-label text-accent mb-4">How They Work</div>
      <h2 id="how-heading" class="font-display text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold text-ink tracking-wide mb-8">
        How Spiral Conveyors <span class="text-accent">Work</span>
      </h2>
      <div class="text-lg text-ink-muted leading-relaxed space-y-6">
        <p>A spiral conveyor carries a conveying surface, typically a slat chain, plastic modular belt, or wire mesh belt, along a helical track around a central column or drum. The track rises (or descends) at a constant pitch. Products loaded at the bottom travel up through the helix and discharge at the top, or the reverse for down-spirals.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink">Drive Types</h3>
        <p><strong class="text-ink">Drum-driven (friction) spirals:</strong> a rotating central drum or cage drives the belt's inner edge by friction. This design is common in spiral freezers, coolers, and proofers, where the belt carries product through many tiers inside an enclosure.</p>
        <p><strong class="text-ink">Positive-drive spirals:</strong> a drive unit engages a slat chain or modular belt directly and pulls it through the helix. This design is common in spirals that move cases, totes, and packaged goods between levels. Ask which drive a quote uses, because it affects belt tension, wear parts, and maintenance access.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink">Spiral Geometry</h3>
        <p>Key dimensions are outside diameter, belt width, number of tiers (wraps), rise per tier, and total elevation change. Pitch must be gentle enough that products do not slide or tip, and the inside radius must suit product length so long items do not overhang or jam. Tighter spirals save floor space but limit belt width and product size. Suppliers publish these limits for their standard models.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- Modular Spirals -->
  <section class="py-20 bg-surface" aria-labelledby="modular-heading">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="section-label text-accent mb-4">Construction</div>
      <h2 id="modular-heading" class="font-display text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold text-ink tracking-wide mb-8">
        Modular vs. Custom <span class="text-accent">Spirals</span>
      </h2>
      <div class="text-lg text-ink-muted leading-relaxed space-y-6">
        <p>A modular spiral conveyor is assembled from standard, factory-built components: tier sections, support columns, a drive unit, and infeed and discharge ends. Height changes by adding or removing tiers, and the unit can be moved or reconfigured when the line changes. The tradeoff is that sizes are limited to the supplier's standard widths, diameters, and pitches.</p>
        <p>Custom-engineered spirals are designed around one application, such as a spiral freezer sized to its enclosure or a spiral for unusually heavy or long products. They fit cases that standard modules cannot, at the cost of more engineering time and less flexibility later.</p>
        <p>"Modular spiral" is also used for spirals that run on <a href="/conveyor-types/modular-conveyors/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-hover font-semibold">modular plastic belt</a>. Confirm which meaning a supplier intends.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Supports and Structure -->
  <section class="py-20 bg-surface-dim bg-dots" aria-labelledby="supports-heading">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="section-label text-accent mb-4">Structure</div>
      <h2 id="supports-heading" class="font-display text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold text-ink tracking-wide mb-8">
        Spiral Conveyor <span class="text-accent">Supports</span>
      </h2>
      <div class="text-lg text-ink-muted leading-relaxed space-y-6">
        <p>Most spiral conveyors are self-supporting: a central column or drum and a ring of outer posts carry the helical track, and the base anchors to the floor. No building steel is needed in most installations, but the support design still drives floor loading, footprint, and access. Check these points before layout is final:</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-3 ml-2">
          <li><strong class="text-ink">Floor slab:</strong> confirm slab capacity for the load concentrated at the spiral base and the supplier's anchor pattern.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-ink">Height and bracing:</strong> tall units, units that pass through a mezzanine or floor opening, and units in seismic zones may need lateral bracing to building structure. Local building code governs.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-ink">Infeed and discharge ends:</strong> define who supplies the support stands and how the top discharge ties into the mezzanine or overhead conveyor.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-ink">Access and guarding:</strong> outer post spacing and guard panels determine where crews can reach the belt. Guard infeed, discharge, and drive pinch points per <a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.212" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-hover font-semibold">OSHA 1910.212</a> and <a href="https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/b20-1-safety-standard-conveyors-related-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-hover font-semibold">ASME B20.1</a>.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-ink">Frame material:</strong> painted or galvanized steel for dry areas. Stainless steel with open, cleanable framing for washdown.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Spiral Up and Down -->
  <section class="py-20 bg-surface" aria-labelledby="updown-heading">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="section-label text-accent mb-4">Up &#x26; Down</div>
      <h2 id="updown-heading" class="font-display text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold text-ink tracking-wide mb-8">
        Spiral Up vs. <span class="text-accent">Spiral Down</span>
      </h2>
      <div class="text-lg text-ink-muted leading-relaxed space-y-6">
        <h3 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink">Spiral Up Conveyors</h3>
        <p>Spiral up conveyors lift cases from a floor-level packaging line to an overhead conveyor, a mezzanine palletizing area, or an elevated shipping conveyor. They need more drive power than spiral down units because they work against gravity plus friction.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink">Spiral Down Conveyors</h3>
        <p>Spiral down conveyors lower products from an overhead conveyor or mezzanine to floor-level shipping. In <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-hover font-semibold">food processing</a>, the long spiral path gives hot products extended dwell time in cooled or ambient air.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink">Dual Spirals</h3>
        <p>Dual spiral conveyors combine an up-spiral and a down-spiral, nested or side by side, to provide both elevation change and a return path in one footprint. They suit processes where products go up through an overhead zone (cooling, drying, curing) and return to the original line.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Continuous vs Indexed -->
  <section class="py-20 bg-surface-dim bg-dots" aria-labelledby="mode-heading">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="section-label text-accent mb-4">Operating Modes</div>
      <h2 id="mode-heading" class="font-display text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold text-ink tracking-wide mb-8">
        Continuous vs. <span class="text-accent">Indexed</span> Operation
      </h2>
      <div class="text-lg text-ink-muted leading-relaxed space-y-6">
        <h3 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink">Continuous Flow</h3>
        <p>Continuous-flow spirals run at constant speed with products entering and exiting simultaneously. Throughput equals belt speed divided by product pitch. For example, a belt at 100 FPM carrying products on 18-inch pitch delivers about 67 products per minute. Mass-flow spirals carry unspaced product, such as bottles or cans, back to back rather than on a set pitch.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink">Indexed Operation</h3>
        <p>Indexed spirals stop and start to fill the spiral before advancing. This maximizes the number of products in the spiral at any time and the holding time per product. It suits cooling and freezing applications where dwell time matters more than throughput rate.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink">Variable Speed</h3>
        <p>Variable-speed drives adjust spiral speed to upstream production rate and downstream demand. The spiral runs faster during peak production and slower when longer dwell time (cooling, drying) is needed. This is common in bakery spiral coolers where rate changes between products.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Applications -->
  <section class="py-20 bg-surface" aria-labelledby="apps-heading">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="section-label text-accent mb-4">Applications</div>
      <h2 id="apps-heading" class="font-display text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold text-ink tracking-wide mb-8">
        Spiral Conveyor <span class="text-accent">Applications</span>
      </h2>
      <div class="text-lg text-ink-muted leading-relaxed space-y-6">
        <p><strong class="text-ink">Packaging line elevation changes:</strong> spirals lift finished cases, cartons, and totes from floor-level packaging to mezzanine palletizing or overhead <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/sortation/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-hover font-semibold">sortation systems</a>, using far less floor space than a long <a href="/conveyor-types/incline-conveyors/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-hover font-semibold">incline conveyor</a>.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-ink">Food cooling and freezing:</strong> spirals carry bakery products, prepared meals, and meat through cooling or freezing chambers. The spiral path provides long dwell time inside a compact enclosure. Stainless steel frames and food-grade belts are standard.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-ink">Distribution center level changes:</strong> spirals move totes and cartons between floors in multi-level distribution and fulfillment centers, where batch-lifting VRCs would limit throughput.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-ink">Vertical buffering:</strong> a spiral holds a long run of conveyor in a small footprint, so slowing or stopping it accumulates product in the helix. Dynamic buffer spirals vary their storage length with line conditions. See <a href="/conveyor-types/buffer-conveyors/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-hover font-semibold">buffer conveyors</a> for FIFO and LIFO buffer designs and <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-hover font-semibold">accumulation conveyors</a> for horizontal options.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-ink">Proofing and drying:</strong> spirals carry dough through temperature- and humidity-controlled proofers and carry coated or washed products through drying zones. The helix maximizes dwell time per unit of chamber volume.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-ink">Clean room and pharmaceutical:</strong> enclosed stainless spirals move product between clean room levels or between production and packaging areas at different elevations.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Selection Guide -->
  <section class="py-20 bg-surface-dim bg-dots" aria-labelledby="selection-heading">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="section-label text-accent mb-4">Specification Checklist</div>
      <h2 id="selection-heading" class="font-display text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold text-ink tracking-wide mb-8">
        How to Specify a <span class="text-accent">Spiral Conveyor</span>
      </h2>
      <div class="text-lg text-ink-muted leading-relaxed space-y-6">
        <p><strong class="text-ink">Elevation change and floor space set diameter and tier count.</strong> Total rise divided by rise per tier gives the number of tiers. A larger diameter allows more rise per tier and wider belts but uses more floor space.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-ink">Product size and weight set belt width and type.</strong> The product must fit the belt with side clearance, and load per foot must stay within the belt and drive rating. Heavy products may call for a heavier-duty positive-drive spiral or a <a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-reciprocating/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-hover font-semibold">VRC</a>.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-ink">Throughput sets belt speed and product pitch.</strong> Products per minute equals belt speed divided by pitch (product length plus gap). <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-hover font-semibold">Transfer conveyors</a> at infeed and discharge must match spiral speed for smooth handoff.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-ink">Environment sets materials and belt type.</strong> Dry packaging: steel frame, slat or plastic modular belt. Washdown food: stainless frame, open belt for drainage. Freezer: stainless frame with a belt rated for the operating temperature. High heat: heat-rated belt and lubrication.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-ink">Supports and access need early decisions.</strong> Confirm slab capacity, bracing, and who supplies infeed and discharge supports. Specify removable guards or walk-in access for belt cleaning and replacement, since belt replacement on a tall spiral is a major maintenance event.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <section class="py-20 bg-surface" aria-labelledby="faq-heading">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h2 id="faq-heading" class="font-display text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold text-ink tracking-wide mb-10">
        Frequently Asked <span class="text-accent">Questions</span>
      </h2>
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <details class="group border border-border bg-surface p-6 open:pb-8" open>
          <summary class="font-display text-xl font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink cursor-pointer list-none flex items-center justify-between group-open:mb-4">
            What is a spiral conveyor?
            <svg class="w-6 h-6 text-ink-muted group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform flex-shrink-0 ml-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"></path></svg>
          </summary>
          <div class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">
            <p>A vertical conveyor that moves products between levels along a helical belt path around a central column. Products enter and exit at the same time, so flow stays continuous. See the <a href="/conveyor-types/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-hover font-semibold">conveyor type guide</a> for all vertical transport options.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="group border border-border bg-surface p-6 open:pb-8">
          <summary class="font-display text-xl font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink cursor-pointer list-none flex items-center justify-between group-open:mb-4">
            What is a modular spiral conveyor?
            <svg class="w-6 h-6 text-ink-muted group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform flex-shrink-0 ml-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"></path></svg>
          </summary>
          <div class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">
            <p>A spiral assembled from standard tier sections, columns, a drive unit, and infeed and discharge ends. Height changes by adding or removing tiers, and the unit can be relocated. Sizes are limited to the supplier's standard range. The term also describes spirals that run on modular plastic belt, so confirm the meaning.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="group border border-border bg-surface p-6 open:pb-8">
          <summary class="font-display text-xl font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink cursor-pointer list-none flex items-center justify-between group-open:mb-4">
            How are spiral conveyors supported?
            <svg class="w-6 h-6 text-ink-muted group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform flex-shrink-0 ml-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"></path></svg>
          </summary>
          <div class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">
            <p>Most are self-supporting: a central column or drum and outer posts carry the track, and the base anchors to the floor. Tall units, units passing through a mezzanine, and units in seismic zones may need bracing to building structure. Confirm slab capacity, anchoring, and infeed and discharge supports.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="group border border-border bg-surface p-6 open:pb-8">
          <summary class="font-display text-xl font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink cursor-pointer list-none flex items-center justify-between group-open:mb-4">
            Spiral conveyor vs VRC, which should I choose?
            <svg class="w-6 h-6 text-ink-muted group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform flex-shrink-0 ml-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"></path></svg>
          </summary>
          <div class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">
            <p>Spiral for continuous, high-throughput flow of cases, cartons, and totes. <a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-reciprocating/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-hover font-semibold">VRC</a> for pallets and heavy loads, lower throughput, and a smaller footprint. Spirals cost more but eliminate batch-cycling delays.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="group border border-border bg-surface p-6 open:pb-8">
          <summary class="font-display text-xl font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink cursor-pointer list-none flex items-center justify-between group-open:mb-4">
            How much does a spiral conveyor cost?
            <svg class="w-6 h-6 text-ink-muted group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform flex-shrink-0 ml-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"></path></svg>
          </summary>
          <div class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">
            <p>Spirals are a significant capital investment compared with incline conveyors or VRCs. Cost depends on elevation, belt width, tier count, drive type, environment (washdown, freezer), and construction material. They justify cost through continuous throughput and small footprint. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-hover font-semibold">conveyor cost guide</a> for detailed factors.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

